#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157103 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "d6fce272-8b91-53f1-bfa4-32f098d8ee86" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-7836" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $x^2e^{x-1} - 2x e^{x-1} -15 e^{x-1} = 0$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}e^{x - 1} - 2xe^{x - 1} - 15e^{x - 1} &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 2x - 15)e^{x - 1} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0$. This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\nx^2 - 2x - 15 = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad e^{x-1}=0\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $x^2 - 2x - 15 = 0$ can be factored as\n$(x+3)(x-5)=0$,\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,5$.\n- The equation $e^{x-1}=0$ has no solutions because $e^{x-1}$ is positive for every real number $x$.\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-3,5$, and their product is\n\n$$\n(-3) \\cdot 5 = -15\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157104 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "f1a4b9b1-a6fd-504b-882f-df32959c35ee" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-7838" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $9x^2\\cdot 3^{x+1} = 16\\cdot 3^{x+1}$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9x^{2} \\cdot 3^{x + 1} &= 16 \\cdot 3^{x + 1} \\\\\n9x^{2} \\cdot 3^{x + 1} - 16 \\cdot 3^{x + 1} &= 0 \\\\\n(9x^{2} - 16)3^{x + 1} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0$. This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n9x^2 - 16 = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad 3^{x+1}=0\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $9x^2 - 16=0$ can be factored as\n$(3x+4)(3x-4) = 0$,\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3},\\dfrac{4}{3}$.\n- The equation $3^{x+1}=0$ has no solutions because $3^{x+1}$ is positive for every real number $x$.\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{4}{3},\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nand their product is\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{4}{3} = -\\dfrac{16}{9}\n$$"}]}
